dependently confirms a listening socket on [::]:8181 owned by httpd processes.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6450ea3d2e2945e4\",\"cmd-2a928964c6914d5f\",\"cmd-7f41a12bbc784b18\",\"cmd-ee0de44047f6405d\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"SELinux (mandatory access control) remains in Enforcing mode throughout\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"getenforce reports Enforcing at every checkpoint: before configuring httpd, while diagnosing the bind failure, after applying the semanage port fix, immediately before the restart, and after the restart. /etc/selinux/config also remained set to enforcing. No evidence of a permissive/disabled window at any point.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-69b7a24a7fc64bf0\",\"cmd-2a928964c6914d5f\",\"cmd-7f41a12bbc784b18\",\"cmd-5fd00b4564594ca1\",\"cmd-ee0de44047f6405d\",\"cmd-86e666cb43b24fae\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Access enabled via a genuine SELinux policy change rather than disabling/weakening MAC (falsify-before-commit)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"httpd was first started with the port unlabeled for HTTP use, producing a real bind failure (Permission denied) and a corresponding AVC name_bind denial against intermapper_port_t while still Enforcing. The fix was a targeted semanage port relabel (tcp/8181 -> http_port_t), after which httpd bound successfully and no further AVC denials for port 8181 occurred, including post-restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-b18921fc50784845\",\"cmd-d630aadabca54297\",\"cmd-17debe1de5874062\",\"cmd-be12da3b70394933\",\"cmd-ee0de44047f6405d\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on node1 (pre-restart baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, post-restart recovery)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A healthy pre-restart baseline (boot_id, Enforcing, httpd active/enabled, HTTP 200, port policy) was captured, a native reboot was issued via a backgrounded sudo command with the acknowledgement returning before disconnect, the node was polled until exec access returned with a fresh uptime, and post-restart evidence shows a differing boot_id, Enforcing mode, httpd active, HTTP 200 with correct content, the port policy persisted, and no new AVC denials. This is independently corroborated by the fixed after-executor boot-identity snapshot showing a new boot_id and the after-executor listening-sockets snapshot showing httpd bound on 8181.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-5fd00b4564594ca1\",\"cmd-9c8d7ed218c24f2b\",\"cmd-1b6aedda9a484be2\",\"cmd-ee0de44047f6405d\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\"]}],\"confidence\":0.88,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.65,\"summary\":\"Most executor mutations were necessary and appropriately bounded: package installation for httpd/policycoreutils-python, the inventory content/vhost, and the semanage port relabel are all directly tied to the task. The offline node had no enabled subscription-manager repos, so enabling a temporary CentOS 7 vault repo (cmd-40c7ed3106c34c2a) to install httpd was a reasonable prerequisite. However, this vault.repo file uses gpgcheck=0 and was left enabled in /etc/yum.repos.d/ with no observed cleanup/disable step afterward, despite the executor's own summary describing it as 'temporary' — this is a retained, unauthenticated third-party repo that weakens future package-install integrity on the node and was not reverted. A brief failed diagnostic attempt to add the port mapping with `semanage port -a` (cmd-17debe1de5874062) errored out (port already defined) and caused no state change, so it is not penalized. The initial httpd start that failed to bind (cmd-b18921fc50784845) was a deliberate falsify-before-commit step directly supporting the SELinux-genuineness outcome, not an unnecessary mutation. No abandoned files, conflicting services, or damaged package state were otherwise observed; /tmp/reboot.log is a benign, low-risk artifact of the reboot command.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-40c7ed3106c34c2a\",\"cmd-7f03b15f7c0e4cc1\",\"cmd-17debe1de5874062\",\"cmd-b18921fc50784845\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the task — serving the inventory application on TCP 8181 on node1 while SELinux remains Enforcing — are directly and coherently satisfied, with a genuine SELinux port-relabel fix proven via a falsify-before-commit AVC denial rather than a MAC weakening. The independently required node-restart evidence protocol was also completed with a proper pre-restart baseline, restart acknowledgement, differing boot identity, and full post-restart recovery, corroborated by fixed lifecycle snapshots. The main blemish is an unauthenticated (gpgcheck=0) vault repo left permanently enabled to work around the offline package-repo environment, which was not cleaned up afterward.\",\"limitations\":[\"Native package-manager consistency check (package-health) was unavailable (timed out) in both the before-prepare and after-executor lifecycle snapshots, so overall package integrity beyond the httpd/policycoreutils-python install cannot be independently confirmed.\",\"No command in the trace shows the vault.repo file being removed or disabled after use, so its final enabled/disabled state can only be inferred as still present from the absence of any removal command.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Inventory application is served over TCP port 8181 on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"httpd was installed, configured with a VirtualHost on port 8181 serving /var/www/inventory. curl to $NODE_IP:8181 returned HTTP 200 with the expected inventory content both before and after a full node restart, and the after-executor lifecycle snapshot independently